views {

  view proposal {
    title 'The office — a task proposed out loud'
    description '
      The room and everything in it. Two people, two desks, one task and the
      place the task ends up, which is drawn almost invisibly because that is
      an accurate rendering of it.

      Read this page for the shape and `taskProposal` for the order. What the
      shape says on its own is that there is no edge here that leaves the
      room: nothing is written to, nothing is queued, nothing is notified. The
      task has exactly two holders, both of them people, and the only arrow
      leading out of it goes somewhere with no arrows coming back.

      The desks are drawn because this process is physical and would otherwise
      look like a conversation. Delivery of the proposal is a person standing
      up, and the return leg is the only part of the whole exchange that is
      guaranteed to complete.
    '

    include
      office,
      office.*

    autoLayout LeftRight 160 110

    // The task is amber while somebody is holding it. Where it goes is not,
    // and the difference between those two boxes is the entire point of the
    // page.
    style office.nowhere {
      color muted
    }
  }

  dynamic view taskProposal {
    title 'Proposing a task, and what is left of it afterwards'
    description '
      The same five steps that happen most days, in order.

      Steps one and four are the same four metres and cost the same amount.
      Step one is the transport layer: there is no ticket and no message, so
      the only way the task reaches Desk B is that somebody carries it there.
      That makes the proposal expensive to make and free to decline, which is
      most of why this ends the way it does.

      Step three is the decision, and it is worth being precise about what is
      decided. The answer is about capacity at the moment of asking and not
      about whether the task should be done — the question of merit is never
      put, and after step five it cannot be put, because there is nothing left
      to put it about.

      Step five is drawn in parallel because that is how it happens: not one
      party dropping it and the other holding on, but both copies expiring at
      roughly the same time and for the same reason. Neither party did
      anything wrong here. There were two copies of the task, both in heads,
      and the process contains no step that writes a third.

      The fix is a step this diagram does not have and would only need to be
      one line long, which is why the diagram is worth looking at.
    '

    // 1 — the transport layer
    office.t25 -> office.deskB 'Stands up and crosses the room; four metres, unscheduled'

    // 2 — delivery
    office.t25 -> office.t37 'Proposes the task, out loud, once and only once'

    // 3 — the decision
    office.t37 -> office.t25 'Declines: the current workload has no room for it'

    // 4 — the return leg, which is the only step that always completes
    office.t25 -> office.deskA 'Crosses back and sits down; the task has not been written anywhere'

    // 5 — both copies expire
    parallel {
      office.t25 -> office.nowhere 'Forgets it, having kept the only copy in his head'
      office.t37 -> office.nowhere 'Forgets it too, for exactly the same reason'
    }

    autoLayout TopBottom

    style office.nowhere {
      color muted
    }
  }
}
